<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      The test APK's own permission, and deliberately **not** the library's.

      `superplayer-moq` has no `src/main` manifest and gains none here: a library that declares
      INTERNET imposes it on every app that merges it, and this module's own code opens a socket
      only because a consumer handed it a `moq://` URI to open. An app playing a MoQ broadcast
      already declares INTERNET for the rest of its networking, and one that does not is an app
      this module would be doing nothing for. That is ADR-0007 rule 4's reasoning — a permission
      that changes what an app may do is the app's to declare — applied to the one permission this
      phase needs.

      It is declared here because the instrumentation APK really is the thing opening the QUIC
      session, so it really is the thing that needs the permission.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
      The watchable half, for a person rather than for an assertion (`MoqPlaybackActivity`'s KDoc
      says why it is here and not in the demo, and why it is not Phase 14's exit criterion).

      `exported` so it can be started with `am start` from a shell, which is the only way anybody
      runs it. It is in the **test** APK, which is installed by `connectedAndroidTest` and by
      nothing a consumer ships.
    -->
    <application>
        <!--
          No `screenOrientation`: the first broadcast this played was 3520x3520 from a fisheye
          camera, and pinning an orientation would be an assumption about a rendition's shape that
          ADR-0018 makes nowhere else.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name="com.superplayer.moq.MoqPlaybackActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="MoQ live" />
    </application>

</manifest>
